The template carries no part of the scan: only the positions read off
it.

What was read, by us, on 2026-10-12, and kept in trace.json beside the
build (our own reading, given away under CC0 1.0):
- the 13 pumps, each named by the street beside it on the map, as Snow
  named none of them;
- the death bars: found by detect.py (beside the build), which keeps ink
  at least 4 pixels thick (Snow's bars are about 25 by 5 pixels; the
  map's lines, letters and the dashes of its boundary line are thinner),
  counts the bars of each stack by its ink against one bar's, and spreads
  that many points over the stack. What the eye decided is in the
  reading: the "PUMP" labels and six marks that are not bars (a stain,
  four dashes of the boundary line, a smudge) left out, and two bars the
  detector missed added. Of five small stacks checked bar by bar, three
  were out by one bar (two under, one over); the total is 580 bars in
  281 stacks. The count Rusty Dodson made of the same map in 1992, published
  by Waldo Tobler, is 578: we use it only as a check, not as data;
- 17 street junctions on the map, matched to today's streets.

The map is laid on the ground with one affine transform fitted by least
squares through the 17 junctions (the OpenStreetMap section below). The
junctions sit 5.6 m from where the fit puts them, as a root mean square
(9.1 m at most, at Lower John Street and Golden Square); left out one at
a time, 7.1 m. Snow's Broad Street pump lands 4.0 m from the red granite
kerbstone that marks the pump's site in Broadwick Street today, and
4.5 m from the replica pump beside it. One junction was left out: on the
map Poland Street and Cambridge Street (today Lexington Street) meet
Broad Street in line, while today they are staggered.

The bars stand where Snow drew them: a deep stack reaches back from the
street into the house, so a death's point is the bar's place on the map,
not an address.


